No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since this is a class library **without DI**, the lightest approach depends on your .NET version:

### Recommended: `TimeProvider` (built-in .NET 8+)

This is the simplest path — no custom wrappers needed. Just inject `TimeProvider` via the constructor:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

Production callers pass `TimeProvider.System`. Tests use `FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
// NuGet: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 30, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 2, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

No DI container required — you're just passing a constructor argument.

> **Pre-.NET 8?** Install the `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` NuGet package for the same API.

